---
import training from '../../../models/tiny/training.json';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import TrainingFigure from '../components/figures/TrainingFigure.tsx';
import Ref from '../components/mdx/Ref.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';
import { chanceLoss, strongestHead, type TrainingData } from '../lib/training';

const data = training as TrainingData;
const snaps = data.snapshots;
const first = snaps[0];
const last = snaps[snaps.length - 1];
const pct = (p: number) => `${Math.round(p * 100)}%`;
const steps = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
// When remembering Fluffy appears: the last saved step under 1%, the next one, and the first past
// half. A new run that never crosses these fails the build instead of printing wrong numbers.
const pastHalf = snaps.find((s) => s.recall >= 0.5);
const lastLow = pastHalf && snaps.findLast((s) => s.recall < 0.01 && s.step < pastHalf.step);
const nextUp = lastLow && snaps[snaps.indexOf(lastLow) + 1];
if (!pastHalf || !lastLow || !nextUp)
  throw new Error('training.json: remembering Fluffy never jumps');
const copyPeak = snaps.reduce((a, b) => (b.copy > a.copy ? b : a));
const head = strongestHead(last.induction);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Watch it learn"
  description="Slide through the tiny model's training and watch its guesses, stories and skills appear."
>
  <section class="prose page">
    <h1>Watch it learn</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      We trained the model again with the same recipe and saved it {data.snapshots.length} times
      along the way. Slide back to the start, where it knows nothing, and forward to the end.
    </p>
    <Figure
      caption="Every number comes from a saved copy of the model at that step."
      description="A slider moves through saved copies of the model during training. For each one it shows the loss on a chart, a story the model writes, its guesses after a sentence, how well it uses earlier text, and a table of how much each attention head copies."
    >
      <TrainingFigure client:visible data={data} chance={chanceLoss(4096)} />
    </Figure>

    <h2>What changes</h2>
    <p>
      Training shows the model a batch of text, measures how surprised it was by each next token,
      and nudges every number a little to be less surprised next time. That surprise is the
      <strong>loss</strong>. At the start the model guesses blindly among 4,096 tokens, a loss of
      {first.valLoss.toFixed(2)}. By the end it is {last.valLoss.toFixed(2)}.
    </p>
    <p>
      The story shows the order things are learned in. First common tokens like "." and "the", then
      words that go together, then whole sentences about a boy named Tim. Using what came earlier in
      the text comes later, and fast. Remembering Fluffy from the first sentence is under 1% after{' '}
      {steps(lastLow.step)} steps, {pct(nextUp.recall)} by {steps(nextUp.step)}, and past half by{' '}
      {steps(pastHalf.step)}.
    </p>

    <h2>Heads that copy</h2>
    <!-- prettier-ignore -->
    <p>
      Some attention heads learn a simple trick. When a token shows up again, they look at the token that came right after it last time, and pass that along as a guess. These are called induction heads. In models of many sizes, they appear at the same point in training as a sudden jump in using earlier text (<Ref id="olsson2022" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      The table measures this on random tokens repeated twice. A head that spreads its weight evenly
      scores about {data.inductionChance.toFixed(2)}. In our small model the best head, layer{' '}
      {head.layer + 1} head {head.head + 1}, ends at {head.score.toFixed(2)}, about{' '}
      {Math.round(head.score / data.inductionChance)} times that. Random tokens are nothing like the
      stories it learned from, so the skills above show the copying more clearly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Skills can also fade. Copying the made-up word Zog peaks at {pct(copyPeak.copy)} after{' '}
      {steps(copyPeak.step)} steps, and is back to {pct(last.copy)} at the end.
    </p>

    <p class="note">
      This is a second run of the recipe in the{' '}
      <a href={withBase('learn/introduction/')}>chapters</a>, so its last copy is close to the model
      the rest of the site uses, but not the same file.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-block: var(--space-12);
  }
  .lede,
  .note {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }
</style>
